  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nHighly amplified pattern exists over the western two thirds of the \nCONUS with deep troughing approaching southern California and a \nmassive ridge building over the Plains and upper Great Lakes. The \n500 mb ridge is forecast to reach maximum amplitude over the local \narea this evening at nearly 580 dam, 99 percentile with respect to \nclimatology. The CA trough will go negatively tilted as it ejects \nacross the northern Plains Wednesday night, then pass across the \nUP/Lake Superior as it opens up Thursday. This trough is not \nassociated with anything resembling a cold airmass, and once it \npasses, the ridge re-builds for Friday into Saturday. \n\nAt lower levels, persistent southwesterly flow will continue to \nresult in an unseasonably warm airmass over the local area this week \nas a surface low tied to the midlevel trough moves from the northern \nRockies through southern Canada Tuesday through Thursday, while an \nanticyclone remains anchored over the central Appalachians. This \nsetup favors highs ranging 15-20F above normal (highs mainly in the \n50s). Record highs of 53F on Thursday (1963) and 56F on Saturday \n(1990) at WFO MQT appear to be at risk based on recent guidance. \nThese days look to be the warmest of the bunch, as we will be within \nthe strongest pressure gradient due to surface lows passing to the \nnorth. Wind gusts of 25-35 mph will be common on Thursday and \nSaturday as well, up to 45 mph over the Keweenaw on Thursday. The \nforecast has trended drier for Thursday with the better forcing \nremaining well to the north.\n\nRain probabilities increase Saturday as a bit more substantial \nmoisture advects northward ahead of the incoming positively tilted \ntrough (PWATs around 0.8\" per the latest LREF). 18Z ensemble surface \nlow clusters strongly favor a track north of the UP, favoring liquid \np-types. Behind this front, there is fairly good confidence in \ntemperatures falling back near seasonal normals by Sunday into early \nnext week. There is a moderate potential that this cool down will be \naccompanied by lake effect snow, although the potential for greater \nthan 2 inches is only about 25% per the latest NBM. Cluster analysis \nshows any more significant snow outcomes would be tied to pieces of \nnorthern stream vorticity being able to dive into the main trough, \nresulting in a secondary low spinning up on the front. That would \nhave the effect of slowing the system down so the cold air could \ninteract with the deeper moisture. Such a solution is considered \nunlikely at this time.",
